-5847-8774-824fd0acc49e" "answer-cc2412c2-8719-51bb-b6b7-0306c809ad0c" "answer-8a949f21-41a5-50c7-b8ef-8bde9e4f1f8c" "answer-6b08898c-ca33-508f-8c94-152e1f94f295"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ec7b01d-43ff-5f69-8032-67aae8fc8563" :answer/id #uuid "7ec7b01d-43ff-5f69-8032-67aae8fc8563" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90cf53c4-54bc-5847-8774-824fd0acc49e" :answer/id #uuid "90cf53c4-54bc-5847-8774-824fd0acc49e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc2412c2-8719-51bb-b6b7-0306c809ad0c" :answer/id #uuid "cc2412c2-8719-51bb-b6b7-0306c809ad0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a949f21-41a5-50c7-b8ef-8bde9e4f1f8c" :answer/id #uuid "8a949f21-41a5-50c7-b8ef-8bde9e4f1f8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b08898c-ca33-508f-8c94-152e1f94f295" :answer/id #uuid "6b08898c-ca33-508f-8c94-152e1f94f295" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186075016 :tutorial/content "We can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following product: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac 13 \\times 2\n$$\n\nFirst, we represent $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ using a model:\n\n![](images/f0/f08054646558ab944940f1f8f0d8a1077748f9882039e775c6cbdd4d307db102.png)\n\n \n\nMultiplication by a whole number is simply a repeated addition. And, since we need to multiply $\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{3}$ by ${\\color{blue}2},$ we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}2$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/31/31fcef6d631262e49fc9c9297fd006f935fd7ee8182b0074d77ae22b626efbbc.png)\n\n \n\nThere are ${\\color{blue}2} \\times {\\color{red}1} = 2$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2 \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186075017 :tutorial/content "There's another way we can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the multiplication problem below:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10} \\times 7\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model to represent $\\dfrac{1}{10}.$\n\n![](images/62/62b50e658ed9bc691a9559548d09f87fbd3b33e007e81b5e78a3235ac4bc3280.png)\n\n \n\nNow, rather than repeating our model $7$ times (which would take a long time), we can instead multiply the number of shaded pieces by $7.$ \n\nOur model has $1$ shaded piece. So, the resulting model must have $1 \\times 7 = 7$ shaded pieces: \n\n![](images/2a/2a7d54b1d7a6a6013ee64b601d9dc0ba31c8a25dae0f5f69abba4b0fed0e8ee2.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\\times 7 = \\dfrac{7}{10}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073982 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
